Root cause:
The bug was caused by an incorrect loop condition in the `get_phy_c45_ids` function, which short-circuited the iteration through available MMDs when `devs_in_pkg` was zero. This prevented the function from probing for device identifiers, resulting in the `c45_ids->device_ids` being populated with zeroes and causing the PHY to be incorrectly probed by the Generic PHY driver.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = 1; i < MDIO_MMD_NUM && devs_in_pkg == 0 &&
     (devs_in_pkg & 0x1fffffff) == 0x1fffffff; i++) {
```
```c
if (!(devs_in_pkg & (1 << i)))
    continue;
```
Reason:
The first statement short-circuits the loop when `devs_in_pkg` is zero, preventing any probing for device identifiers. This results in `c45_ids->device_ids` being populated with zeroes. The second statement further ensures that if `devs_in_pkg` remains zero, the loop will skip probing for any devices, leading to incorrect behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (i = 1; i < MDIO_MMD_NUM && (devs_in_pkg == 0 ||
     (devs_in_pkg & 0x1fffffff) == 0x1fffffff); i++) {
```
```c
if ((devs_in_pkg & 0x1fffffff) == 0x1fffffff)
    return -ENODEV;
```
Reason:
The first statement modifies the loop condition to allow iteration even when `devs_in_pkg` is zero, enabling the function to probe for device identifiers. The second statement checks if `devs_in_pkg` is still all ones after probing, allowing the function to exit gracefully if no devices are found, thus ensuring proper handling of the probing process.


